   <dc:title>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Fabric Phase Sorptive Extraction Prior to HPLC--DAD Determination of Monomers Released from Dental Resin Composites in Dental Wastewater</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Fabric Phase Sorptive Extraction Prior to HPLC--DAD Determination of Monomers Released from Dental Resin Composites in Dental Wastewater</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:title>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>dental resin composites; fabric phase sorptive extraction (FPSE); high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC); dental wastewater; BPA; TEGDMA; UDMA; Bis-GMA</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
   <dc:type>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Text</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:type>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">A novel fabric phase sorptive extraction method (FPSE) was developed for the detection of monomers released during dental restorative procedures</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">A novel fabric phase sorptive extraction method (FPSE) was developed for the detection of monomers released during dental restorative procedures</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">which are subsequently discharged into the environment through dental unit drainage systems. The presence of Bisphenol-A (BPA)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">which are subsequently discharged into the environment through dental unit drainage systems. The presence of Bisphenol-A (BPA)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Triethylene glycol dimethacrylate (TEGDMA)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Triethylene glycol dimethacrylate (TEGDMA)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Urethane dimethacrylate (UDMA) and Bisphenol A-glycidyl methacrylate (Bis-GMA)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Urethane dimethacrylate (UDMA) and Bisphenol A-glycidyl methacrylate (Bis-GMA)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">released from dental resin composites in dental wastewater</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">released from dental resin composites in dental wastewater</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">was monitored using high-performance liquid chromatography coupled to diode array analysis (HPLC--DAD). FPSE was optimized with respect to the following significant parameters: (a) membrane type</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">was monitored using high-performance liquid chromatography coupled to diode array analysis (HPLC--DAD). FPSE was optimized with respect to the following significant parameters: (a) membrane type</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">(b) membrane size</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">(b) membrane size</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">(c) extraction time</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">(c) extraction time</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">(d) stirring during extraction</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">(d) stirring during extraction</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">(e) sample volume</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">(e) sample volume</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">(f) ionic strength</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">(f) ionic strength</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">(g) elution solvent</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">(g) elution solvent</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">(h) elution time</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">(h) elution time</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and (i) agitation during elution. For HPLC--DAD analysis</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and (i) agitation during elution. For HPLC--DAD analysis</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">the mobile phase constituted of acetonitrile--water</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">the mobile phase constituted of acetonitrile--water</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and a gradient elution program was followed. The detection was carried out at 220 nm. The method was validated in terms of linearity</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and a gradient elution program was followed. The detection was carried out at 220 nm. The method was validated in terms of linearity</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">sensitivity</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">sensitivity</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">selectivity</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">selectivity</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">accuracy</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">accuracy</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and precision</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and precision</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">showing satisfactory results. The limits of detection (LOD) and quantification (LOQ) were equal to 0.015 ng/$\upmu $L and 0.05 ng/$\upmu $L, respectively</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">showing satisfactory results. The limits of detection (LOD) and quantification (LOQ) were equal to 0.015 ng/$\upmu $L and 0.05 ng/$\upmu $L, respectively</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">for BPA</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">for BPA</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">TEGDMA</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">TEGDMA</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and Bis-GMA</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and Bis-GMA</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and 0.04 ng/$\upmu $L and 0.13 ng/$\upmu $L</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and 0.04 ng/$\upmu $L and 0.13 ng/$\upmu $L</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">respectively</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">respectively</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">for UDMA. The method demonstrated satisfactory precision</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">for UDMA. The method demonstrated satisfactory precision</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">with relative standard deviation (RSD) values lower than 7.7% for intra-day and lower than 9.5% for inter-day studies. Relative recoveries ranged from 93.9% to 100.8% (intra-day) and from 92.0% to 101.7% (inter-day) for all target analytes. The developed FPSE--HPLC--DAD method was applied to the filtered/dissolved fraction of a real dental wastewater sample after centrifugation and filtration.</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">with relative standard deviation (RSD) values lower than 7.7% for intra-day and lower than 9.5% for inter-day studies. Relative recoveries ranged from 93.9% to 100.8% (intra-day) and from 92.0% to 101.7% (inter-day) for all target analytes. The developed FPSE--HPLC--DAD method was applied to the filtered/dissolved fraction of a real dental wastewater sample after centrifugation and filtration.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
